WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.640
- I'm Olivia Newman, director
of "Where the Crawdads Sing,"

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.777
and this is "Tricks of the Trade."

00:00:04.777 --> 00:00:07.277
(light music)

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:14.940
What's funny is,

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.930
I have this distinct memory
of hearing her interviewed

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.030
about the book on the radio.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.140
And I remember,

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.440
as I was listening to
her describe the story,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.600
I just thought, "Oh my god, this is,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.510
this sounds so my kind of world."

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.830
And then at the end of the interview,

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.140
they mentioned that Reese had
picked it for her book club,

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.087
and I just, my heart just
sank because I thought,

00:00:37.087 --> 00:00:38.910
"Well, she's gonna go make it into a movie

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.650
and I'm gonna read it and I'm gonna wish

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.090
that I could turn it into a movie."

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.000
Then, fast forward a year or so later,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.550
my agents told me they
were looking for a director

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.027
for this book "Where the Crawdads Sing."

00:00:49.027 --> 00:00:49.980
"Have you read it?"

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.395
I said, "Are you kidding me?

00:00:51.395 --> 00:00:55.380
That's been on my top of my shelf to read

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:56.640
and I just, I've been too worried

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.800
about my heart getting broken. (laughing)"

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.210
But I ready it in two days.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.590
I could not put it down.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.510
And it was exactly what
I expected it to be.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.670
I was do drawn into Kya's story.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.910
Delia's writing was so
lyrical and evocative

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.840
and really put me in
this very specific place

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.100
through the eyes of a character

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.410
that I had never quite seen before.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.560
And I just, I was dying to
see it come to life on screen.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.440
And then after I read the
book, I red Lucy's script,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.240
and I was really excited to work with her

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.720
because she just felt like
the perfect screenwriter

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.280
to attach to this.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.480
Being from the South

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.570
and also being such a
naturally lyrical writer,

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.410
she really was able to kind
of channel Delia's voice

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:41.070
into the script and be as
faithful as she could to the book.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.537
So, it was just amazing,

00:01:43.537 --> 00:01:45.723
the way that it kind of came full circle.

00:01:46.993 --> 00:01:49.470
(light music)

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.380
I mean, I think that when I read the book

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.290
and thought about the
movie version of the book,

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.340
I wanted to make sure

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.920
that I captured the emotional experience

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.530
that the book takes you
on more than anything.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:05.400
It's this epic story that
weaves an entire person's life,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.910
and it's heartbreaking,
but it's also hopeful.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.800
There's this driving mystery to it

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.690
that kind of keeps you turning the pages

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:14.340
'cause you're dying to
know, did she do it,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.173
didn't she do it?

00:02:15.173 --> 00:02:18.900
There are these romances that
are kind of also keep you

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.090
at the edge of your seat
to see what's gonna happen

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:23.100
with these different men that she meets.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.170
So I wanted to make sure that the movie

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.690
followed that same sort
of emotional journey.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.330
And then, I think what's
the biggest difference

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.010
between the movie and the book

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.550
is that I had the feeling
that I really wanted

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.090
as much as possible for us
to be in Kya's point of view

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.990
and to follow the story in her shoes.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.220
I didn't wanna leave her.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.090
And so, rather than have
the courtroom drama happen

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:49.260
towards the end of the movie,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.090
I wanted to weave it throughout.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.030
And so, we set the trial up
right from the beginning.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.950
And so that way, whenever
you're in the present,

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.420
you're with Kya on trial

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.307
and you're sort of on the
edge of your seat wondering,

00:03:00.307 --> 00:03:01.740
"Is she gonna get off or not?"

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.570
And then whenever you're
in the past, of course,

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.940
you're following her tale of survival

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.430
and romance and coming of age.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.288
That differs a bit from
the structure of the book.

00:03:11.288 --> 00:03:14.490
(light music)

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.170
We had 12 weeks of prep.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:20.070
The first priority was finding
a spot to build Kya's house.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.230
Because the first priority was making sure

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.260
that we could shoot it on location

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.350
in a setting where we could
move from inside the house

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.390
to outside and we could bring
the marsh into the house.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.300
We didn't wanna have to
shoot her house on a stage.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.200
And I really admire my
production designer Sue Chan,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.650
who found this location in a state park

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.650
where when you looked one
direction out into Kya's lagoon,

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:44.850
it really took your breath away.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.010
You really felt like you were in the book

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.200
looking out into the kind of landscape

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.920
that Kya would have sat at
her lagoon and looked out at.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.390
But when you looked the other way,

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.570
it was wide open, there
was this historic house,

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.370
the Otis Mansion, right there.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.370
And then there were all
these big live oaks.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.830
And I just said, "Well Sue,
this is never gonna work.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:06.270
How are we gonna cover all this?"

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.400
And she said, "No, no, no,
we're gonna get greens.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:09.570
We're gonna build the house here

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:10.980
and it's gonna block a certain amount.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.510
And then we're gonna fill in with greens."

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:15.720
And I said, "But what about
the path that Ma walks down?

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.130
We have to have a path.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.680
That image from the book was just,

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.870
is one of those images
that stays with you."

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.370
And she said, "No, no, we're gonna,

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.640
we'll use the curve of the
branches of the oak trees

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.650
and we'll create this path under them."

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.990
So it was just one of those
like cross your fingers

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.190
and hope it all works out,

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:33.870
and it was magnificent.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.520
Still, when I watch the movie,

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.580
I just sit there going, "I
cannot believe (laughing)

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.990
how remote it looks."

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:42.210
The book was definitely our North Star.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.280
We returned to it over and over again

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.370
for passages that describe the landscape

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.470
because Delia is just
such an amazing writer,

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:53.460
especially when it comes to
capturing nature and wildlife.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:54.870
And it was important that we find

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.870
all of the different
textures to Kya's world.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:00.390
And then, Barkley Cove, I would say,

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.780
was probably the hardest
location for us to recreate.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.310
It just is hard to find towns on the coast

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.850
right close to the beach
like that that still exist,

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.320
that haven't been developed.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.080
When we decided to shoot
this film in New Orleans,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.810
we were looking for towns
that at least had architecture

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.610
that was of the time period

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:24.660
and also felt small enough
that you could imagine a town

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.510
where everybody would be
gossiping about the Marsh Girl

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.490
and that everybody knew each other

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:32.310
and there was that sort of
sense of an intimate space.

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.680
So, we looked at a lot of different towns,

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.360
and they just felt too big.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.750
We really looked high and low
for the right kind of town,

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:41.400
and we found it in Houma,

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:45.180
which has these storefronts
that really feel plucked

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.670
right out of the '50s.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.190
It feels very untouched.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.560
All we had to do was
really dress the windows,

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.420
bring in picture cars,

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:57.720
hide some modernities, but it
was just absolutely perfect

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.053
in terms of time period.

00:06:00.053 --> 00:06:03.450
(mysterious music)

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.400
We couldn't shoot in an actual fire tower.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.100
We couldn't get all of our
equipment up that high.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:08.970
It wasn't safe.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.790
So we had to build that and
we had to green screen that,

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:14.130
and we actually found a fire tower

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:16.620
that was built in the exact place

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.690
where we would imagine this
fire tower would be built,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.400
overlooking the marsh lands,

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.380
that wasn't even accessible by road.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.060
It was only accessible by boat.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.910
And so, we went out to
that location with a drone

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:30.030
and we shot plates from
that same fire tower

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.490
so that you really felt the right height.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:35.100
We used it as a model for
the fire tower that we built.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.680
So it's funny because if we could've shot

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.690
on the top of that fire
tower, we would've.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:43.650
I would say, shooting on
boats was really challenging.

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.110
Not only because like
anytime you shoot on boats,

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.900
your crew is just,
you're a fleet of boats.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.550
You're not one boat.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.890
There's the picture boat, and
then there's the camera boat,

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:55.650
and then there's hair and
makeup is on a different boat,

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.770
and props is on, and it's
you're this fleet going out.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.890
But the bayous that we were shooting in,

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:03.660
the water would rise and fall.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:07.800
And so, there were times
where the waters just fell

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.900
to a point where it was too shallow.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.580
So we would go out scouting

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:15.090
and we'd find a location
where we would set the scenes

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:16.730
and we'd, Polly and I would shot list it

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:19.950
in a very specific way based
on what it looked like,

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:21.810
and then the day before
we were set to shoot,

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:23.670
we'd find out the water was too low

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:26.730
and we were gonna have to
find another spot on the day.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.330
And we'd just get out there an
hour before the cast arrived

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:32.490
and be going around in
boats finding a place

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:33.390
that was deep enough,

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.910
that sort of still had the
same look that we needed.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:38.251
So, that was challenging.

00:07:38.251 --> 00:07:41.310
(mysterious music continues)

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:43.980
There's so much story to fit into a movie.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.110
Kya's relationship to the marsh

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.450
and what she takes from
it is hugely important.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.270
It doesn't have the driving
force of the mystery

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.320
and of the romances.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.380
I mean, those are really
kind of what keep you engaged

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.213
in the story.

00:07:56.213 --> 00:07:59.640
But understanding Kya
as a character requires

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.160
that you spend time with her in the marsh,

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:05.880
you spend time with her observing
nature and her drawings.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:09.180
And so, we had a B
camera unit that went out

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.430
and filmed the marsh whenever we knew

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:12.840
we weren't gonna need a second camera.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:16.500
We were very meticulous
about scheduling days

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.780
and looking at when we were
gonna be on certain locations

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.300
where we knew you would
be able to get egrets

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.580
and great blue herons and make sure

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.370
that there was enough time
to get all of that wildlife

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:28.440
so that we could incorporate
it into the film.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.890
And then, for me, one of
the most emotional parts

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.280
of the film is the moment that Ma leaves

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:36.960
and it's so vividly described.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.000
I mean, I think it happens
on page one in the book.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.440
I really wanted to make sure

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.780
that we got those details across,

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.480
the sound of the screen door slamming,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.620
the snakeskin heels,
and her blue suitcase.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:52.710
Those details that really
just stick in your mind.

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:54.840
You have this image that you can't shake,

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.977
and there were things like
that that I just thought,

00:08:56.977 --> 00:08:59.730
"We can't ever get those details wrong."

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:03.270
And there was a question
about the vision that Kya has

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:05.730
of her mother, whether it needed
to be in the movie or not,

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:08.340
and to me, it felt like
such an important part

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:13.110
of her whole story arc was
that vision that she has.

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:17.228
And in the book, that was the
moment where I just lost it,

00:09:17.228 --> 00:09:19.470
(laughing) and so I
really wanted to make sure

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:21.030
that we captured that.

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:24.420
I mean, the other thing
that was hard to balance

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.090
in an adaptation was,
there's so much history

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:29.610
that you learn about
Kya's parents in the book

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.830
and where they're from
and trying to figure out

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:33.810
how much of that we
could get into the movie.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:38.810
And we, I think, where
we tried to layer that in

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.490
was through the production design.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:44.100
Her mother comes from some
money from New Orleans,

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:47.010
and what we imagined when
we were building the house

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:51.120
and designing it was that perhaps
Ma brought some furniture,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.910
had some antiques, and at first tried

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:55.200
to make the house really pretty,

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:57.420
even though it was just
this fishing cabin.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.940
And so, there's peeled wallpaper

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.880
that you can imagine Ma put up
and one wall that's painted,

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:04.650
but now the paint is peeling.

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:09.030
And just remnants of a
life that started out there

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:12.660
with hope that her mother
passed onto her this idea

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:14.670
that even if you don't have a lot,

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.670
you can take pride in where
you live and what you wear

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.920
and you can still take care of yourself

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.030
and carry yourself with dignity.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:26.370
I think was really important
to try to get through.

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:29.940
There's so much in the
dialogue that is a reflection

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:30.773
of the character.

00:10:30.773 --> 00:10:33.450
So the way that Kya speaks
when she's a teenager,

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:34.710
before she's learned to read

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:37.440
and before she's really
immersed herself in literature,

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:40.950
is very different from once
she's educated herself.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:43.620
So, and Delia just does
such an amazing job

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:46.500
with capturing that particular
voice and its evolution.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.650
So, going back to the book
again to look at the way

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:51.390
that Delia did that was important.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:54.390
And of course, Lucy added
her own embellishments

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:57.240
and sometimes we would
have to add information

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.270
that you can describe in
a novel in a different way

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.400
than you can have to show in a movie.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.460
And Delia, again, she
created these characters

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:09.450
and really, there's not a
single piece of dialogue

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:13.395
that I'm sure Delia didn't think
about and write and rewrite

00:11:13.395 --> 00:11:14.730
for a reason.

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:18.540
And so, as much as we could
honor what she did so well,

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:22.590
we would take directly from
the book, and then, of course,

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:25.350
if things needed to be
changed or altered slightly,

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.870
that was in Lucy's wise hands.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:30.900
Of course, there's a poetry
woven throughout the book,

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:33.540
both in the dialogue and
in Kya's observations.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:37.110
And whenever we could
use it for the voiceover

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:38.760
or use it in the dialogue

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:41.340
and where it felt natural
and it made sense,

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.583
we would always go back
to the source for sure.

00:11:43.583 --> 00:11:46.916
(light music continues)

